WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.170
Buckle up everyone

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:04.463
because I just learned something weird
and now you all need to know it too.

00:00:04.463 --> 00:00:07.184
I was on a call the other day with a woman
named Emily Fairfax.

00:00:07.184 --> 00:00:09.000
And she knows a lot about beavers.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.850
Yes, beavers.

00:00:11.374 --> 00:00:14.034
And at one point, she just
casually mentions...

00:00:14.034 --> 00:00:17.590
a weird period of beaver relocation in the 40s

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:21.169
where we dropped them out of airplanes.

00:00:21.169 --> 00:00:22.493
I’m sorry. What?

00:00:22.493 --> 00:00:24.369
There's this absolutely absurd video.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:27.000
You can find it on YouTube from 
Idaho Fish and Game.

00:00:29.657 --> 00:00:32.098
"Now into the air and down they swing."

00:00:32.569 --> 00:00:38.010
"The box opens and a most unusual and novel
trip ends for Mr. Beaver."

00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:43.474
Okay, the method is super questionable.

00:00:43.474 --> 00:00:47.920
But the Idaho Department of Fish and Game
did this to relocate 76 beavers

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.170
away from humans and into a remote location
where there were no roads.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.865
Because beavers build dams.

00:00:53.865 --> 00:00:57.072
Structures that completely alter the landscape.

00:00:57.072 --> 00:01:00.682
But now, research has found that beavers and
their dams do so much more

00:01:00.682 --> 00:01:02.250
than flood the forest.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:07.325
As the planet continues to warm they might
also be vital in cooling parts of it down.

00:01:13.202 --> 00:01:15.299
So when beavers move into a stream

00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:17.643
their very first order of business is 
to build that dam

00:01:17.643 --> 00:01:20.469
to start making a little bit of a safe habitat
for themselves.

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:23.939
They start with some logs or stones to secure
the structure.

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:28.390
After that they’ll keep piling sticks, stones,
and mud over and over again

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:30.622
until water starts to pool behind it.

00:01:31.931 --> 00:01:36.020
As flowing water pushes against the dam
it begins to carve out a deep pool

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.392
which is great for the beaver because on land...

00:01:38.392 --> 00:01:42.420
They're pretty unfortunately awkward
but once they get into the water

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.751
that's where they really shine and excel.

00:01:44.751 --> 00:01:47.292
But it’s also ideal for other species

00:01:47.292 --> 00:01:50.938
because deeper water tends to be much cooler
than water at the surface.

00:01:50.938 --> 00:01:53.460
So if you're a fish and it's
a really hot day

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.970
you can go down into one of these deep pools
and just hunker down at the bottom

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.950
where the water is coolest and has the least
sunlight affecting it.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.550
The dam also forces circulation:

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.429
as water hits the dam, some of it is 
forced into the soil

00:02:06.429 --> 00:02:10.500
where it mixes with cold ground water 
before resurfacing.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.120
As a result, one study found that water 
near beaver dams

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.661
is up to 4 degrees Fahrenheit (2.3 degrees Celsius) cooler
than water elsewhere.

00:02:18.572 --> 00:02:22.494
This comfortable little pond is the first
defense against predators

00:02:22.494 --> 00:02:25.590
but beavers will also dig out canals into
the surrounding land

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.912
which function as a small highway system 
for them.

00:02:28.912 --> 00:02:32.852
All this tampering with the stream spreads
the water around the area.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:36.061
As that water evaporates, the water vapor
chills the air.

00:02:36.061 --> 00:02:41.939
At one of my sites in central California,
I went to visit on a particularly hot day.

00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:43.865
It was 107 Fahrenheit (42 Celsius).

00:02:43.865 --> 00:02:45.260
And then I get into the complex

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.040
and I hold up the temperature probe and I'm
like wow it's 91 (33 Celsius).

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.180
We lost almost 15 degrees of air temperature

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.549
just going into this beaver complex.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:56.700
The area becomes a wetland ecosystem

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.780
and on first glance it’s a total mess with branches,
plants, and water everywhere.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:02.980
And that’s a good thing.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:07.382
A river or a riverscape should be very complex,
very messy.

00:03:07.382 --> 00:03:10.780
And it's that complexity that makes them
so resilient to climate change

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.399
and all sorts of other disturbances.

00:03:12.399 --> 00:03:15.122
And that resiliency is increasingly important.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:23.879
In the summer of 2022, heatwaves and fires
plagued parts of Europe and the US.

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:28.530
As the climate crisis worsens we can expect
these events to increase.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.376
Beavers - it turns out - might be able to
help.

00:03:31.376 --> 00:03:34.340
Because these wetland ecosystems 
are especially important

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.576
in areas that are prone to fire or drought.

00:03:37.576 --> 00:03:41.599
Dams slow water down and allow it to seep
deep into the Earth

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:44.840
which provides a source of groundwater 
for humans.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:50.448
It also keeps the surrounding land wet
and wetter land is less likely to burn.

00:03:50.448 --> 00:03:56.799
This is an image from Idaho in 2018 when 65,000
acres were scorched by wildfire.

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:01.000
This green patch in the middle is a wetland
created by a beaver dam.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.819
Emily and a colleague studied the effects
of this in 2020

00:04:03.819 --> 00:04:07.463
and found repeated evidence that beaver dams
create firebreaks.

00:04:07.463 --> 00:04:11.197
Providing a safe place for animals to go when
wildfires break out.

00:04:11.197 --> 00:04:14.870
That was the first study in the scientific
literature that said

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.199
beaver ponds are too wet to burn in many cases.

00:04:20.751 --> 00:04:22.438
Beavers have had bad PR.

00:04:22.438 --> 00:04:27.100
They’re often considered annoying pests
because wetland creation is messy.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.801
When a dam is built in the wrong place it
can flood roadways or homes.

00:04:30.801 --> 00:04:34.470
And previously, the solution was extermination

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.434
but that seems to be changing.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:39.110
California, for example, just passed a bill
that requires

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.790
state sponsored beaver-relocation.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.645
Don’t worry... they won’t be dropping
them out of planes.

00:04:45.129 --> 00:04:48.350
What we’re learning is that beavers do some
of the best engineer work

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.730
for climate resiliency.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:52.560
We just have to let them.

00:04:53.005 --> 00:04:55.239
We've got to let go of a little bit of control.

00:04:55.239 --> 00:04:57.200
Especially once a beaver starts building.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.250
What was a pretty cute little stream becomes
this super wild, messy wetland.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:02.330
Like, that's good.

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:03.530
Remember that that is good.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.143
We want it to be messy.

